      {
        "id": "def-qft-poincare-test-action",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Proper-orthochronous Poincaré action and test pullback",
        "statement": "Fix coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and the Minkowski matrix\n$$\\eta:=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1).$$\nThe proper-orthochronous Lorentz group is\n$$SO^+(1,3):=\\{\\Lambda\\in GL_4(\\mathbb R):\\Lambda^{\\mathsf T}\\eta\\Lambda=\\eta,\\ \\det\\Lambda=1,\\ \\Lambda^0{}_0\\ge1\\},$$\nwith its subspace topology from real $4\\times4$ matrices. The proper-orthochronous\nPoincaré group is the semidirect product\n$$P^\\uparrow_+:=SO^+(1,3)\\ltimes\\mathbb R^4,$$\nwith multiplication $(\\Lambda,a)(\\Gamma,b)=(\\Lambda\\Gamma,a+\\Lambda b)$,\nidentity $e=(I,0)$, and affine action $gx=\\Lambda x+a$. Its topology is the\nproduct topology on the matrix and translation coordinates.\n\nFor $f\\in S:=\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$ define the pointwise pullback\nformula, viewed initially as a smooth function,\n$$T_gf(x):=f(g^{-1}x)=f(\\Lambda^{-1}(x-a)).$$\nOn smooth functions this formula obeys $T_gT_h=T_{gh}$ and $T_ef=f$, by the\ngroup law. This definition specifies the formula and conventions; it does not\nassert that $T_gf$ belongs to $S$. Translations have the convention\n$T_{(I,z)}f(x)=f(x-z)$.\n\nWith this signature, $p\\cdot z:=p^0z^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf z$,\n$\\overline V_+:=\\{p\\in\\mathbb R^4:p^0\\ge|\\mathbf p|\\}$ is the closed forward\ncone, and $x-y$ is spacelike exactly when\n$(x^0-y^0)^2-|\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y|^2<0$.",
        "strategy": "Definition fixing the Lorentz signature, group topology, affine action, and pullback convention.",
        "deps": [
          "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jens Mund, General Aspects of Relativistic Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, DF-UFJF (2012), §2.1",
              "url": "https://www.ufjf.br/fisica/files/2022/03/AQFT.pdf",
              "locator": "PDF pp. 12–17: proper-orthochronous Poincaré covariance and its pullback action on test functions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Christopher J. Fewster and Kasia Rejzner, Algebraic Quantum Field Theory—an introduction, arXiv:1904.04051v2 (2019)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04051",
              "locator": "§4.1, printed pp. 13–15: the proper-orthochronous Poincaré group and its action in the QFT framework."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-minkowski-fourier-transform",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Minkowski Fourier transform and forward-cone support convention",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), as\nrequired by the Fourier transform suppliers. With $p\\cdot z$ from\n[[def-qft-poincare-test-action]], for $\\psi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ set\n$$\\mathcal F_M\\psi(z):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}e^{-ip\\cdot z}\\psi(p)\\,d^4p.$$\nThis is a continuous automorphism of Schwartz space. Indeed, if\n$\\mathcal F_{2\\pi}$ is the Euclidean transform\n$\\mathcal F_{2\\pi}\\psi(x)=\\int e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot p}\\psi(p)\\,d^4p$,\nas in [[def-fourier-transform-of-a-tempered-distribution]], then\n$$\\mathcal F_M\\psi(z)=\\bigl(\\mathcal F_{2\\pi}(\\psi\\circ\\eta)\\bigr)(z/(2\\pi)).$$\nThe reflection $\\eta$ and dilation by $1/(2\\pi)$ preserve Schwartz space by\n[[lem-invertible-linear-substitutions-preserve-schwartz-space]], and\n$\\mathcal F_{2\\pi}$ is a topological automorphism by\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]].\n\nFor $D\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$ define its Minkowski Fourier transform by\nbilinear transposition,\n$$\\langle\\widehat D_M,\\psi\\rangle:=\\langle D,\\mathcal F_M\\psi\\rangle\\qquad(\\psi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nFor a closed set $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, the statement\n$\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat D_M\\subseteq C$ means\n$\\langle\\widehat D_M,\\psi\\rangle=0$ for every compactly supported smooth\n$\\psi$ whose support is disjoint from $C$; the inclusion\n$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4)\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is\n[[lem-test-function-inclusion-in-schwartz-space-is-continuous]]. In particular,\nthe forward-cone condition means $C=\\overline V_+$ with the definition in\n[[def-qft-poincare-test-action]]. No joint self-adjoint energy-momentum\ngenerators are included in this convention.",
        "strategy": "Definition by the stated Minkowski phase and bilinear transposition; the Schwartz automorphism relation is displayed.",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-poincare-test-action",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-tempered-distribution",
          "def-fourier-transform-of-a-tempered-distribution",
          "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space",
          "lem-invertible-linear-substitutions-preserve-schwartz-space",
          "def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn",
          "lem-test-function-inclusion-in-schwartz-space-is-continuous"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-poincare-test-action": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-fourier-transform-of-a-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-invertible-linear-substitutions-preserve-schwartz-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-test-function-inclusion-in-schwartz-space-is-continuous": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jens Mund, General Aspects of Relativistic Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, DF-UFJF (2012), §2.1 and proof of Theorem 2.6",
              "url": "https://www.ufjf.br/fisica/files/2022/03/AQFT.pdf",
              "locator": "PDF pp. 13–18: the Fourier-support spectrum condition and Poincaré convention; the transform convention below is stated explicitly to fix sign and normalization."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "library": "mathematics"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-poincare-pullback-continuity-on-schwartz",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Proper-orthochronous Poincaré pullbacks act continuously on Schwartz space",
        "statement": "For every $g\\in P^\\uparrow_+$, the pullback $T_gf(x)=f(g^{-1}x)$ of\n[[def-qft-poincare-test-action]] maps $S=\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$\ncontinuously to itself. For each fixed $f\\in S$, the map\n$g\\mapsto T_gf$ from $P^\\uparrow_+$ to $S$ is continuous. Consequently,\n$\\alpha_g(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n):=T_gf_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes T_gf_n$\nextends algebraically to a unital star-automorphism of the algebraic tensor\ntest algebra [[def-qft-positive-tempered-moments]], and\n$\\alpha_g\\alpha_h=\\alpha_{gh}$. No topology on the algebraic direct sum is\nasserted.",
        "strategy": "Direct Schwartz-seminorm estimates for affine pullbacks and continuity in the group parameter.",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-poincare-test-action",
          "def-qft-positive-tempered-moments",
          "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-poincare-test-action": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-positive-tempered-moments": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jens Mund, General Aspects of Relativistic Quantum Field Theory, lecture notes, DF-UFJF (2012), §2.1",
              "url": "https://www.ufjf.br/fisica/files/2022/03/AQFT.pdf",
              "locator": "PDF pp. 12–17: proper-orthochronous Poincaré pullback on test functions in the Wightman framework."
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "library": "mathematics",
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-tempered-field-reconstruction",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Tested positive moments reconstruct closable smeared fields",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) and let\n$(W_n)_{n\\ge0}$ be a positive tempered moment family on $T(S)$ as in\n[[def-qft-positive-tempered-moments]]. Write $\\omega$ for its linear extension,\nand let $P^\\uparrow_+$, $T_g$, and $\\alpha_g$ be the group and pullbacks of\n[[def-qft-poincare-test-action]] and\n[[lem-qft-poincare-pullback-continuity-on-schwartz]]. Let\n$(H_\\omega,D_\\omega,\\pi_\\omega,\\Omega_\\omega)$ be the GNS construction of\n[[thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain]] for this functional, with the first-variable-\nlinear Hilbert pairing.\n\nFor $f\\in S$, put $X(f):=f$ in degree one and\n$\\Phi(f):=\\pi_\\omega(X(f))|_{D_\\omega}$. Then $D_\\omega$ is a common invariant\ndense domain, every $\\Phi(f)$ is closable with\n$\\Phi(\\overline f)=\\Phi(f)^*|_{D_\\omega}$, and $\\Phi$ is a weak\noperator-valued tempered distribution in the exact sense of\n[[def-qft-weak-operator-valued-tempered-distribution]]. Its vacuum moments are\nexactly the input:\n$$\\bigl(\\Phi(f_1)\\cdots\\Phi(f_n)\\Omega_\\omega,\\Omega_\\omega\\bigr)=\\langle W_n,J_n(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n)\\rangle.$$\n\nThe following additional conclusions are conditional on additional moment\nidentities.\n\n1. If $\\omega\\circ\\alpha_g=\\omega$ for every $g\\in P^\\uparrow_+$, then there\nis a strongly continuous unitary representation $g\\mapsto U_g$ on\n$H_\\omega$ fixing $\\Omega_\\omega$ and satisfying\n$$U_g\\Phi(f)U_g^{-1}|_{D_\\omega}=\\Phi(T_gf)\\qquad(g\\in P^\\uparrow_+,\\ f\\in S).$$\n\n2. Let $f,h\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C)$ be\nthe complex test functions of [[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]],\nviewed as elements of $S$ under the canonical inclusion of\n[[lem-test-function-inclusion-in-schwartz-space-is-continuous]]. In this\ndefinition, $\\operatorname{supp}f$ and $\\operatorname{supp}h$ are the closures\nin $\\mathbb R^4$ of their nonzero sets and hence compact. If these supports\nare spacelike separated, meaning $(x^0-y^0)^2-|\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y|^2<0$ for every\n$x\\in\\operatorname{supp}f$, $y\\in\\operatorname{supp}h$, and\n$$\\omega\\bigl(b^*[X(f),X(h)]a\\bigr)=0\\qquad(a,b\\in T(S)),$$\nthen $[\\Phi(f),\\Phi(h)]=0$ on $D_\\omega$. More generally, any polynomial\nrelation $r\\in T(S)$ satisfying $\\omega(b^*ra)=0$ for all $a,b\\in T(S)$ is\nrepresented by $\\pi_\\omega(r)=0$ on $D_\\omega$; tested field equations are\ninstances of this statement when their required identities are assumed.\n\n3. For translations $\\tau_z=(I,z)$ define\n$$D_{b,a}(z):=\\omega\\bigl(b^*\\alpha_{\\tau_z}(a)\\bigr)\\qquad(a,b\\in T(S)).$$\nIf $\\omega$ is translation-invariant, each $D_{b,a}$ is a bounded continuous\nfunction and hence a regular tempered distribution; applying the\nsymmetry-implementation lemma to the translation subgroup gives strongly\ncontinuous unitaries $U_{\\tau_z}$. If, in addition,\n$\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat{D}_{b,a,M}\\subseteq\\overline V_+$ for every $a,b$,\nwith the Fourier convention of\n[[def-qft-minkowski-fourier-transform]], then the reconstructed translation\nmatrix coefficients\n$$z\\longmapsto\\bigl(U_{\\tau_z}\\pi_\\omega(a)\\Omega_\\omega,\\pi_\\omega(b)\\Omega_\\omega\\bigr)$$\nhave precisely the same Fourier support. This is a matrix-coefficient\nspectrum condition; it does not assert joint self-adjoint energy-momentum\ngenerators or their joint spectral measure.\n\nThis theorem reconstructs a weak field only from supplied, actually positive\ncontinuous moments. It does not construct an unspecified positive functional,\ncomplete a formal perturbation series, or establish the full Wightman\nreconstruction theorem with assumptions not listed here.",
        "strategy": "Direct conditional GNS reconstruction, matrix-coefficient continuity, and support transfer under the displayed moment identities.",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-positive-tempered-moments",
          "def-qft-weak-operator-valued-tempered-distribution",
          "def-qft-poincare-test-action",
          "def-qft-minkowski-fourier-transform",
          "lem-qft-poincare-pullback-continuity-on-schwartz",
          "thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain",
          "lem-qft-positive-state-cauchy-schwarz",
          "lem-qft-state-symmetry-implementer",
          "thm-polynomial-growth-functions-define-tempered-distributions",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "lem-test-function-inclusion-in-schwartz-space-is-continuous"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-positive-tempered-moments": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-weak-operator-valued-tempered-distribution": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-poincare-test-action": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-minkowski-fourier-transform": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-poincare-pullback-continuity-on-schwartz": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain": "mathematical-premise",
